\f1\fs22 The Chuglinghams\'92 barbecue wound down as the sun rested on Narrow Neck Plateau separating Jamison Valley from Megalong Valley farther west. Hayley invited Kathy for a walk down to the look-out. Together they watched the last splash of sunset colours fade from the sky. The awkwardness that had shadowed them throughout the afternoon persisted, amplified by the vast open spaces before them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So,\'92 Hayley said as she cast her vision far into the valley, \'91stasis technology. Makes you wonder what else they're keeping from us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy raised an eyebrow. \'91Still on that?\'92 She wasn\'92t sure whether to close the distance between them or accept the offer of more work talk.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sorry, occupational hazard.\'92 Hayley leaned against the safety railing. \'91Old cop habits. Can't stop questioning things.\'92 She adjusted her weight on her feet and somehow finished a few centimetres closer to Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That's what makes you\'85\'92 Kathy trailed off with a half-smile then looked away.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Makes me what?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy almost regretted not putting on a fresh patch, but she wanted to feel \'85 this. \'91Oh, I dunno.\'92 She shrugged and leaned on the railing too.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Receiving neither compliment nor criticism, Hayley stared away to the south. \'91How's Sarah?\'92 she asked after a few stretched moments.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Patient confidentiality,\'92 Kathy replied automatically, then sighed. \'91Her pregnancy is... progressing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s specific.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It's complicated. She\'92s at once the most exhausted and most radiant expectant mother I\'92ve ever seen, and that\'92s just according to her charts.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91She is radiant,\'92 Hayley agreed and Kathy felt a pang of jealousy. Hayley added, \'91Nothing ever seems to slow her down. I mean, she fought off a professional assassin in an elevator, for tax sake. On her wedding day.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91While carrying a some genetically engineered version of a long-dead\'85\'92 Kathy shook her head, aching for a patch. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Probably nothing any expectant mother wouldn\'92t be capable of,\'92 Hayley said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stood back from the fence and drummed the railing. \'91Ah, yeah, motherhood, eh? Not something I\'92d ever be cut out for.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley tried to meet Kathy\'92s gaze, but she assiduously avoided it. \'91I think you underestimate yourself.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Me?\'92 Kathy blinked the notion away. \'91Nah. Fast times and fun times are all I\'92m good for.\'92 She gulped and became very still. \'91In the end, I\'92ll alway be\'97\'92 A visceral reaction helped her cut off the last of her thought, and she cursed herself for always putting obstacles in the way of those who drew too close.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley pushed her weight off the safety rail and turned for Kathy. \'91Well fast times and fun times are good times too.\'92 She reached for Kathy\'92s elbow. \'91We could start there first.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the first time, Kathy met Hayley\'92s gaze. Where she\'92d expected to see the steely eyes of a professional investigator she saw only the starlight and soft mist of softening eyes. She saw a perfect arch of an eyebrow, the curve of a nose line designed by an artist, the smoothest, firmest, most sensual cheek, and the most minimal of smile that spoke of the forgiveness of all her sins.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I've been thinking about you since the day we met,\'92 Hayley confessed and caressed Kathy\'92s cheek.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy had been thinking in similar fashion about Sarah since the day they\'92d met but felt her resistance dissolving under Hayley\'92s touch. \'91Tax! Let\'92s roll with it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The embraced and kissed. In moments, Hayley was breathless and wide-eyed. At the first opportunity, Kathy thrust her hand under Hayley\'92s blouse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ouch!\'92 Hayley cried, and smacked Kathy\'92s hand away. \'91Typical doctor. Where\'92s your bedside manner?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I save it for when I\'92m bed-side.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I think I like the sound of that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy  grabbed Hayley\'92s hand, and led her up the look-out path.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To Hayley, it seemed the lampposts along the way were there for the sole purpose of leading them to Kathy\'92s bed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s bedroom door suffered the indignity of being flung open with such enthusiasm it bounced off the wall and nearly closed itself again in protest. The two tumbled onto the bed like a pair of acrobats who'd forgotten the rest of their routine but decided to improvise despite the obvious prospect of personal injury.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Wait,\'92 Hayley gasped between kisses, \'91are those glow-in-the-dark star stickers on your ceiling?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Focus, detective,\'92 Kathy murmured, working on Hayley's buttons with the dexterity of someone who'd spent years tying surgical knots. \'91You can investigate my decor later.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Their clothes scattered across the floor as if they\'92d been tossed out of a suitcase from a plane. Hayley's police-issue practicality met Kathy's medical-professional sensibility in a tangle of cotton and synthetic blends that would have made fashion designers cut their own cloth with a pair of mulesing sheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know,\'92 Hayley said, tracing a finger along Kathy's collarbone, \'91for someone who claims to be all about fast times, you\'92re remarkably thorough.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Medical training,\'92 Kathy replied, then continued to demonstrate more thoroughness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If there were Olympic events for enthusiasm and creativity, they would have qualified for several medals, though the judges might have deducted points for some of the extortive language Kathy used and for knocking over the bedside lamp. Twice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The universe, in its infinite wisdom, had apparently decided that two women who spent their days dealing with genetic mysteries and criminal investigations deserved a night where the only mystery was how they'd never done this before, and the only investigation required was thoroughly empirical.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Later, as they lay tangled in the sheets they\'92d put through their own unique spin cycle, Hayley traced lazy circles on Kathy's shoulder. \'91So, doctor, will I live?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy grinned. \'91Uncertain. We might need a few follow-up appointments.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think I can clear my schedule,\'92 Hayley said, pulling her closer. Outside, the night wrapped around them like a discreet curtain. Somewhere close, perhaps in the trees by the back fence, a riot of kookaburras laughed as if they knew exactly what kind of fast and fun times had just transpired.
